WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:03.400
- [Narrator] Intense heat,

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.940
freezing cold,

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.420
high acidity,

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.300
and radioactivity.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.259
These harsh environments
don't seem hospitable for life

00:00:11.259 --> 00:00:15.769
but some organisms not
only survive but thrive

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:18.323
under such extreme conditions.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:23.939
The name extremophile means extreme lover.

00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:27.558
These organisms live in
exceptionally harsh environments.

00:00:27.558 --> 00:00:31.270
Such as hot hyperthermal
vents or buried in rocks

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:34.120
far beneath the Earth's surface.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.590
Extremophiles occur in
all three domains of life.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.559
Bacteria, archaea, and eukaryotes.

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:44.220
They range from the extreme
heat-loving thermomophiles

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.510
which feed off of inorganic chemicals

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.596
and have special enzymes to
survive high temperatures

00:00:49.596 --> 00:00:53.480
to extreme cold-loving
psychrophiles which have evolved

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.580
antifreeze proteins that
help ensure their survival

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.083
in some of the coldest
waters on the planet.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.210
But the most extreme living
things on Earth are tardigrades.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:08.440
Also known as water bears,
these water-dwelling

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.780
micro animals are polyextremophiles.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.990
This means they are capable of surviving

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.210
multiple harsh conditions.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.460
They are nearly indestructible
and have even survived

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.993
the extreme conditions of outer space.

00:01:21.993 --> 00:01:25.720
Tardigrades have a unique
adaptation that allows them

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:29.340
to curl up into a dry,
seemingly lifeless ball.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.180
And slow down their metabolic rate.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:35.302
In this state, they can
survive cold, dry environments

00:01:35.302 --> 00:01:37.783
like space for decades.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:42.750
Studying the adaptations of
extremophiles may hold the key

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.160
to solving many of Earth's problems.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.790
This includes the development

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:49.010
of genetically based medications,

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:51.220
producing new types of biofuels,

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:54.303
and protecting people
against radiation exposure.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.120
Extremophiles have opened
our minds to the many

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.580
possibilities of life and the environments

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.160
that can support it.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.840
Understanding the limits
of life in these extreme

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:08.760
conditions on Earth may
provide scientists with clues

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.603
of how life could possibly
exist elsewhere in the universe.

